      'Relative Pronouns and their Functions: 1- Who: Refers to people as subject or object (The doctor who treated me was kind). 2- Whom: Refers to people as OBJECT only, and can come directly after prepositions (The man whom/who I met / The teacher to whom I spoke). 3- Which: Refers to things, animals, or an entire previous clause (The car which he bought / He passed his exams, which made his parents proud). 4- That: Replaces who or which in defining relative clauses only (NEVER after commas or prepositions). 5- Whose: Shows possession for people, animals, and things (The girl whose phone was lost / The house whose roof collapsed). 6- Where: Refers to places with an action happening inside (The school where I studied). If the place is described or followed by a preposition, use (which): (The school which I visited / The school which I studied in / The school in which I studied). 7- When: Refers to time (The year when he was born). If preceded or followed by a preposition, use (which): (The day on which we met).',
      'Defining vs Non-defining Relative Clauses: 1- Defining Clauses: Provide essential information needed to identify the person or thing; no commas are used; (that) can replace who/which: (The students who worked hard scored high marks). 2- Non-defining Clauses: Provide extra, non-essential information enclosed between two commas (, ... ,); (that) CANNOT be used; the relative pronoun cannot be omitted: (Mr. Taha, who lives next door, is an author).',
      'Omission of Relative Pronouns: The relative pronoun (who, whom, which, that) can be completely deleted ONLY if it is the OBJECT of the relative clause: (The man whom/that I saw yesterday = The man I saw yesterday). But if it is the SUBJECT followed by a verb, it cannot be omitted unless reduced to a participle: (The man who saw the accident = The man seeing the accident).',
