      '1- Rise vs Raise vs Arise vs Arouse: Rise (rose, risen): Intransitive (NO object); to move upwards, increase, or wake up (The sun rises in the east / Prices are rising rapidly). Raise (raised, raised): Transitive (NEEDS an object); to lift up, increase, collect money, or bring up children (Raise your hand / The charity raised funds / She raised three children). Arise (arose, arisen): Intransitive; to emerge, occur, or appear (Problems arise when communication fails). Arouse (aroused, aroused): Transitive; to awaken emotions or suspicions (His strange behavior aroused suspicion).',
      '2- Win vs Beat vs Gain vs Earn: Win (won, won): Win a competition, match, medal, prize, election, or war (Egypt won the cup). Beat (beat, beaten): Defeat an opponent, rival, or team; takes the PERSON or TEAM as object (Ahly beat Zamalek 2-0). Gain (gained, gained): Acquire intangible, abstract things or weight/speed (gain experience, gain knowledge, gain weight, gain reputation). Earn (earned, earned): Receive money for work done (earn money, earn a living, earn a salary).',
      '3- Rob vs Steal: Rob (robbed, robbed): Focus on the VICTIM or PLACE: Rob someone / a bank of something (The gang robbed the bank / They robbed the old man of his watch). Steal (stole, stolen): Focus on the ITEM or MONEY taken: Steal something from someone/place (Someone stole my car from the street).',
      '4- Affect vs Effect: Affect (verb): To influence or make a change to (Pollution affects our health). Effect (noun): An outcome or consequence (Pollution has a bad effect on our health).',
