      '1- Capitalization: Capitalize: First letter of a sentence, proper nouns (Ahmed, Egypt, Nile, Africa), days, months, and holidays (Monday, May, Eid), languages and nationalities (English, Egyptian), titles before names (Dr. Magdi Yacoub, President Sadat), and the pronoun (I) anywhere in the sentence.',
      '2- The Comma (,): Used: a- In a list of three or more items: (I bought apples, bananas, and oranges). b- After introductory words, adverbials, or clauses: (Suddenly, the lights went off. / Although he ran fast, he missed the bus). c- Around non-defining relative clauses: (My uncle, who lives in London, is a pilot). d- Direct address: (Ali, listen to me. / Listen to me, Ali). e- In direct speech before quotation marks: (He said, "I am ready."). f- In question tags: (You are coming, aren\'t you?).',
      '3- The Semicolon (;): Used to link two independent, closely related sentences without a conjunction (FANBOYS): (I have an exam tomorrow; I need to study tonight). Also used before conjunctive adverbs when linking sentences: (He worked hard; however, he failed the exam).',
